From: wsclean-mx <wsclean-mx@localhost>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 22:28:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09/23] Metal gridding: degridding kernel, round-trip benchmark
 and adjoint check

The w-gridder runs both directions, so the harness now covers both and the
property that ties them together.

* grid_degrid_poly (MSL) is the adjoint operation: one thread per visibility, no
  atomics, no tiles, summing the 6x6 neighbourhood with the same ducc0
  polynomial weights; the grid is read as plain floats (the gridding kernel's
  atomic merge is complete by then).
* metaldevice gains gridDegrid() (arbitrary grid in, values out), so the probe
  can feed it a grid the gridding kernel did not produce.
* mx-metal-probe --op roundtrip measures both directions against their float64
  twins and checks the adjoint identity <G x, Y> = <x, D Y> against an
  independent random grid.

Measured (603 814 visibilities, 1024^2, poly-h, min of 5): gridding 12.60 ms,
degridding 10.10 ms, round trip 22.70 ms (26.6 Mvis/s); gridding rel_max 3.0e-6,
degridding rel_max 2.7e-7, adjoint identity rel_diff 4e-8, round trip 10.2x a
scalar double twin.

